<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cesare Maria De Vecchi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1884–1959</span> · Also known as Cesare De Vecchi, Count of Val Cismon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cesare Maria De Vecchi was an Italian Fascist leader, one of the quadrumvirs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-rome">March on Rome</a> in 1922. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Italian Somaliland</a> from 1923 to 1928 he conquered the northern sultanates, and as governor of the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> Islands from 1936 to 1940 he imposed forced Italianisation on schools and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> church. He later voted against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> in 1943.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cesare-maria-de-vecchi.jpg" alt="Cesare Maria De Vecchi" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cesare_Maria_De_Vecchi1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
